7E0:\n```\nD7E0 BE 06 D0   LDX $D006,Y   ; hmm\nD7E3 1B         ...\n```\nHmm that doesn't look right. Let's disassemble from 0xD7E0 properly. Byte sequence starting at index 0:\nBE 06 D0 = LDX $D006,Y (3 bytes)\n1B = ? ($1B is not a valid 6502 opcode... actually $1B is undefined). Hmm, that suggests misalignment. Maybe the routine actually starts at a different address; the bytes before might be data or the routine is at $D7E0 but with a different alignment.\n\nActually wait, maybe read_memory prgRom at \"22496\" is raw ROM offset including header? The mapping says prgRom offsets are \"without the 16-byte iNES header\". And CPU $D80C = prgRom 0x580C. Hmm but the mapping also sometimes says prgRom 0x580C? Actually for the star it said \"CPU $D816\". And the disassemble tool at CPU 0xD80C gave LDA $39, matching index 44 = offset 0x580C. So prgRom address 0x580C = CPU $D80C. Good, direct.\n\nSo the routine above $D80C starts earlier. Let's find the entry: bytes index 32 onwards: 20 98 C9 = JSR $C998; A9 06 = LDA #$06; 20 11 DA = JSR $DA11; A9 20 = LDA #$20; 85 FE = STA $FE; A5 39 = LDA $39 ...\n\nSo the routine at index 32 = offset 22528 = 0x5800 = CPU $D800:\n```\nD800 JSR $C998\nD803 LDA #$06\nD805 JSR $DA11\nD808 LDA #$20\nD80A STA $FE\nD80C LDA $39\n...\n```\nHmm, that seems to be the power-up collection routine. $39 = power-up type, read here. So $39 IS the power-up type. But is it the emerging power-up's type, or the collected one?\n\nLook at the bytes before index 32: index 18-31: 173 159 7 208 8 76 44 217 169 0 157 190 6 96 = \n```\nAD 9F 07  LDA $079F\nD0 08     BNE +8\n4C 2C D9  JMP $D92C\nA9 00     LDA #$00\n9D BE 06  STA $06BE,X\n60        RTS\n```\nThat's the star-flash handling probably (reads star timer $079F). Then index 0-17:\n```\nBE 06 D0  LDX $D006,Y\n1B        ...\n```\nHmm that's odd. Let me re-read: index 0: BE,1:06,2:D0,3:1B,4:A9,5:01,6:9D,7:BE,8:06,9:B5,10:64,11:49,12:FF,13:18,14:69,15:01,16:95,17:64,18:AD...\n\nMaybe the routine starts at index 4:\n```\nA9 01     LDA #$01\n9D BE 06  STA $06BE,X\nB5 64     LDA $64,X\n49 FF     EOR #$FF\n18        CLC\n69 01     ADC #$01\n95 64     STA $64,X\nAD 9F 07  LDA $079F\n...\n```\nYes! That aligns perfectly. So the code before index 4 (BE 06 D0 1B) is the tail of a previous instruction (part of something at $D7DC maybe \"BE 06 D0\" ... hmm). Whatever.\n\nSo $39 is the power-up type variable. Now: does $39 hold the type while the item emerges? The power-up object (mushroom/flower/star) in SMB is object... In SMB, the power-up is stored in the enemy object table? Actually, I recall the \"power-up\" uses enemy object slot with type values: 0? Hmm. But the type variable $39 is one byte (not per-slot), suggesting there's only ever one power-up on screen. Yes! SMB allows only one power-up at a time, and $39 holds its type. That matches: the ? block sets $39 to the item type, the emerging power-up object uses it, and on collection the routine at $D800 reads $39.\n\nSo the plan: hit the ? block, then set $39 = 2 while it's emerging. Then the star will emerge (probably the sprite changes too since the sprite routine reads $39... maybe). Let's test.\n\nBut careful: right now $39 = 2 already. That's leftover. When we hit the block it will be set to the block's item type (likely 0 for mushroom, or maybe $39 set based on logic). Then we change to 2.\n\nActually, if it's the mushroom block, hitting it sets $39 = 0 (mushroom) or maybe the game decides flower vs mushroom at collection. The mapping says $D80C LDA $39 / CMP #2 / BCC $D820, and $D820 LDA $0756 / BEQ → set 1 (big) ... So the type stored is \"mushroom\" (0 or 1) and the big/fire distinction is made at collection based on $0756. Hmm, actually the item type for a mushroom-containing block is 0? and flower is 1? Let's see: $D820 branch handles type<2: if $0756==0 → become big (mushroom). If $0756==1 → become fire (flower). So both mushroom and flower items funnel here; type 0 = mushroom, 1 = flower perhaps, and the \"$0756\" check overrides. Hmm, actually in real SMB, a block that contains a mushroom gives a flower if you're big, and the object type stored is the same (mushroom type) - the game decides. Fine.\n\nSo: set $39=2 after the hit. Let's test. Actually, wait: will the emerging object's sprite be a star or mushroom? The sprite likely depends on $39 too. Doesn't matter for our purposes: we need a \"starman\" (star power-up) on screen, then list_sprites.\n\nBut hmm — is the \"starman\" a metasprite? The star power-up in SMB is a single 16x16 sprite (4 tiles?) that bounces. Let's find out via list_sprites.\n\nHowever, caution: The task says \"make one appear ... pause while it is on screen, and list_sprites on it (the proof)\". So we need the star actually rendered.\n\nPotential issue: If we set $39=2 while the mushroom is emerging, the mushroom object may still be drawn as a mushroom (sprite chosen at spawn). The star sprite might only appear if type is set before the object spawns. Hmm. But the task's hint says changing the type before the item comes out yields a star, implying the sprite follows. Let's just test and see.\n\nAlso, alternatively: We could set $39=2 BEFORE hitting the block. But the block hit sets $39 itself, overwriting. Unless the block only sets $39 if... hmm.\n\nActually here's the thing: the block item type is stored in the *block object* at some address (e.g. $06A1 block type array). The known fact says the item type is decided when the block is hit. So on hit, the game reads the block's content and sets $39. So we must change after the hit.\n\nAlternatively we can change the block's content type before hitting (but that's in the level layout/blocks, harder).\n\nOK, let's proceed.\n\nAlso for the star timer: note that setting $39=2 and collecting gives the star effect. But we just need it on screen.\n\nNow, positioning: Mario at level x 335, the ? block at screen x 218-233 → level x 368-383. Wait, the nametable read at col 28 row 18 had tile $53? Actual